body_text:
### Windows Terminal version

1.24.11911.0 is installed, but the leak was traced in the **inbox `conhost.exe`** (10.0.26100.1, `C:\Windows\System32`). I have not checked whether `OpenConsole.exe` behaves the same way.

### Windows build number

10.0.26200.9457 (Windows 11 Home 25H2, x64). `win32kfull.sys` is 10.0.26100.9444.

### Other Software

Git for Windows 2.55.0.windows.3 (MSYS2 runtime 3.6.9, a Cygwin fork). Any Cygwin/MSYS2 `exec()` triggers it. AI coding agents (Claude Code, Codex) run every shell command through Git Bash, so a developer machine hits this path hundreds of thousands of times.

### Steps to reproduce

The script below needs only Git for Windows and no admin rights. It counts kernel `Token` objects (`NtQueryObject(ObjectTypesInformation)`) before and after a Git Bash loop that fork+execs 300 times. A `cmd.exe` loop is the control.

```powershell
# powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File repro.ps1
param([string]$Bash = "$env:ProgramFiles\Git\usr\bin\bash.exe", [int]$N = 300)
Add-Type @'
using System; using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
public static class ObjCount {
  [DllImport("ntdll.dll")] static extern int NtQueryObject(IntPtr h, int cls, IntPtr buf, int len, out int ret);
  public static long Of(string typeName) {
    int len = 1 << 18, ret; IntPtr b = Marshal.AllocHGlobal(len);
    try {
      if (NtQueryObject(IntPtr.Zero, 3, b, len, out ret) != 0) return -1;
      int n = Marshal.ReadInt32(b, 0); long off = IntPtr.Size;
      for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        IntPtr e = new IntPtr(b.ToInt64() + off);
        short nameLen = Marshal.ReadInt16(e, 0), maxLen = Marshal.ReadInt16(e, 2);
        string name = Marshal.PtrToStringUni(Marshal.ReadIntPtr(e, IntPtr.Size), nameLen / 2);
        if (name == typeName) return (uint)Marshal.ReadInt32(e, IntPtr.Size * 2);
        off += 0x68 + ((maxLen + IntPtr.Size - 1) & ~(IntPtr.Size - 1));
      }
      return -1;
    } finally { Marshal.FreeHGlobal(b); }
  }
}
'@
function Run([string]$exe, [string]$argLine) { $psi = New-Object Diagnostics.ProcessStartInfo $exe, $argLine; $psi.UseShellExecute = $false; [Diagnostics.Process]::Start($psi).WaitForExit() }
function Measure-Case([string]$label, [scriptblock]$body) {
  Start-Sleep 2; $before = [ObjCount]::Of('Token'); & $body; Start-Sleep 5; $after = [ObjCount]::Of('Token')
  '{0,-50} Token objects {1:N0} -> {2:N0} ({3:+#;-#;0})' -f $label, $before, $after, ($after - $before) }
Measure-Case "control: cmd.exe runs $N x where.exe" { Run "$env:SystemRoot\System32\cmd.exe" "/c for /l %i in (1,1,$N) do @`"%SystemRoot%\System32\where.exe`" /q cmd" }
Measure-Case "bash runs $N x /usr/bin/true (fork + exec)" { Run $Bash "-c 'for i in `$(/usr/bin/seq $N); do /usr/bin/true; done'" }
Measure-Case "bash runs $N x ( : ) (fork only, no exec)" { Run $Bash "-c 'for i in `$(/usr/bin/seq $N); do ( : ); done'" }
```

### Expected Behavior

Token objects return to baseline once the processes exit, as they do in the `cmd.exe` control.

### Actual Behavior

Each `exec()` from an MSYS2 process leaves one `Token` object behind. The leaked objects never go away; only a reboot frees them.

| 300 iterations | Token objects |
|---|---|
| control: `cmd.exe` → `where.exe` | −7 to +2 |
