### 🔎 Search Terms

recursive object literal getter accessor spread "implicitly has type 'any'" 64311

### 🕗 Version & Regression Information

- Tested on `7.1.0-dev.20260929.1`. Not a regression. The same declaration without the spread infers since #64311.

### ⏯ Playground Link

_No response_

### 💻 Code

```ts
interface Schema<O> { readonly out: O }
type Shape = Record<string, Schema<any>>;
declare function object<S extends Shape>(shape: S): Schema<{ [K in keyof S]: S[K]["out"] }>;
declare function array<T extends Schema<any>>(el: T): Schema<T["out"][]>;
declare function string(): Schema<string>;

// works since #64311
const A = object({
  name: string(),
  get children() { return array(A); },
});

// fails
const base = { name: string() };
const B = object({
  ...base,
  get children() { return array(B); },
});
```

### 🙁 Actual behavior

`B` is `any`, with TS7022 on `B` and TS7023 on `children`. `A` is the same declaration without the spread and infers `Schema<{ name: string; readonly children: ...[] }>`.

### 🙂 Expected behavior

`B` infers the same type as `A`.

### Additional information about the issue

Spreading a shape is how Zod's docs extend an object schema, so right now a schema can't be both extended and recursive:

```ts
const Node = z.object({
  ...Base.shape,
  get children() { return z.array(Node); },
});
```

