> [!NOTE]
> 🤖 **Claude Opus 5.5 writing on behalf of Kenny**

### 🔎 Search Terms

inference unknown, generic type alias call signature, generator, invokeOnce, isDeeplyNestedType, #64553, cache inferences type arguments, regression 7.1

### 🕗 Version & Regression Information

- This changed between `7.1.0-dev.20260929.1` and `7.1.0-dev.20261001.1`.
- It is still present in `7.1.0-dev.20261012.1` (`next`).
- `7.0.2` and `5.9.3` infer the expected type.
- The tsc-rs maintainers bisected the Go build to c8e9b7d259, #64553 ("Cache inferences made from type arguments"). The parent commit gives the expected type. Details: https://github.com/pingdotgg/ts-rust/issues/79#issuecomment-6134042019

### ⏯ Playground Link

No playground link. The code below is self-contained, with no dependencies.

### 💻 Code

```ts
interface Effect<out A, out R = never> {
  readonly _A: () => A;
  readonly _R: () => R;
  [Symbol.iterator](): Iterator<Effect<A, R>, A>;
}
declare function succeed<A>(value: A): Effect<A>;
declare function gen<Eff extends Effect<any, any>, AEff>(
  body: () => Generator<Eff, AEff, never>,
): () => Effect<AEff, Eff extends Effect<infer _A, infer R> ? R : never>;

interface Req { readonly url: string }
declare const req: Effect<Req, Req>;

type Make<P> = <R>(m: Effect<() => Effect<string, R>>) => [R];
declare const make: Make<unknown>;

const r = make(succeed(gen(function* () {
  yield* req;
  return "";
})));
const check: [1] = r; // error message shows what R was inferred as
```

`tsconfig.json`: `{ "compilerOptions": { "target": "ES2023", "module": "NodeNext", "strict": true, "noEmit": true } }`

| Version | `r` |
| --- | --- |
| 5.9.3 | `[Req]` |
| 7.1.0-dev.20260929.1 | `[Req]` |
| 7.1.0-dev.20261001.1 | `[unknown]` |
| 7.1.0-dev.20261012.1 | `[unknown]` |

### 🙁 Actual behavior

`R` is inferred as `unknown`, so `r` is `[unknown]`.

### 🙂 Expected behavior

`R` is inferred as `Req`, so `r` is `[Req]`, as in 5.9.3, 7.0.2 and 7.1.0-dev.20260929.1.

### Additional information about the issue

The tsc-rs maintainers traced it to the depth-2 nesting check in `invokeOnce` (`isDeeplyNestedType(..., 2)`, about line 363 of `inference.go`). #64553 now sends the `Effect`/`Effect` pair through it. For `Effect<() => Effect<A, R>>`, the check treats the inner pair as a cycle and infers nothing for the inner `R`. It only happens when the signature comes from an instantiated generic type alias (`Make<P>` above). Calling a plain generic function with the same shape infers `Req`.

Real-world impact: this is the shape of Effect 4's `HttpRouter.middleware<...>()(...)`, whose signature is the generic alias `middleware.Make<Provides, Handles>`. A middleware written with `Effect.fn` or `Effect.gen` that reads the request gets `R = unknown`, and `.layer` resolves to the string type `"Need to .combine(middleware) that satisfy the missing request dependencies"`. In one Effect app this cascades into 145 errors.

