### 🔎 Search Terms

narrowing equality literal widening, non-widening literal, narrowTypeByEquality, isUniformUnionType, TS2322 narrowed let, flatMap union inference 7.1

### 🕗 Version & Regression Information

- This changed in commit or PR microsoft/typescript-go#4781 (322fa1ec). I built tsgo at its parent commit (98c47847) and at 322fa1ec. Only 322fa1ec reports the errors below.
- This changed between versions 7.1.0-dev.20260730.1 and 7.1.0-dev.20260731.1. It still reproduces on 7.1.0-dev.20261012.1. TypeScript 6.0.3 and 7.0.2 report no errors ([same code on 7.0.2](https://tsgo.sxzz.dev/#)).

### ⏯ Playground Link

[TypeScript Go Playground, nightly 2026.10.13](https://tsgo.sxzz.dev/#)

### 💻 Code

(`--strict`)

```ts
declare const kind: string | undefined;

if (kind === "a") {
    let copy = kind;
    copy = "b";

    const obj = { kind };
    obj.kind = "b";

    const arr = [kind];
    arr.push("b");
}

declare const count: number | undefined;

if (count === 1) {
    let copy = count;
    copy = 2;
}
```

### 🙁 Actual behavior

```
main.ts(5,5): error TS2322: Type '"b"' is not assignable to type '"a"'.
main.ts(8,5): error TS2322: Type '"b"' is not assignable to type '"a"'.
main.ts(11,14): error TS2345: Argument of type '"b"' is not assignable to parameter of type '"a"'.
main.ts(18,5): error TS2322: Type '2' is not assignable to type '1'.
```

The narrowed `kind` now has the non-widening literal type `"a"`. So `let`, object literal properties and array literals initialised from it are typed `"a"` instead of `string`.

### 🙂 Expected behavior

No errors, matching 6.0.3, 7.0.2 and the commit before microsoft/typescript-go#4781. Inside the `if`, `kind` should have the widening literal type `"a"`, so mutable locations widen to `string`.

### Additional information about the issue

(This bug report was assembled by AI, but I reviewed it prior to posting.)

- I think the cause is the fast path microsoft/typescript-go#4781 added to `narrowTypeByEquality`. When the declared type passes the new `isUniformUnionType` check (a primitive union such as `string | undefined`), it returns `getRegularTypeOfLiteralType(valueType)`, the non-widening form of the literal. `unionContainsType` counts `"a"` as contained in `string | undefined` because the union includes `string`, so this path runs here. The old path, `replacePrimitivesWithLiterals(filteredType, valueType)`, returned the comparison's widening literal. A plain `string` declared type isn't a union, so it isn't affected. Perhaps the fast path should only apply when the union contains the literal itself, but I haven't tested that.
- `switch (kind) { case "a": ... }` already produced a non-widening literal in 6.0.3 and 7.0.2. If `===` matching `switch` is intended, it's still a breaking change. microsoft/typescript-go#4781 is described as a performance optimization, and its top-400-repos run came back clean.
- Real-world impact: in our codebase this broke `flatMap` inference, because the branches of the callback no longer unify:
  ```ts
  declare const requests: Array<{ parameterPath?: string }>;
  requests.flatMap((request) => {
      if (request.parameterPath === "lookupFields") return [{ parameterPath: request.parameterPath }];
      if (request.parameterPath === "recordFields") return [{ parameterPath: request.parameterPath }];
      return [];
  }); // TS2345: '"recordFields"' is not assignable to '"lookupFields"'
  ```

