### 🔎 Search Terms

createSnapshot, openProjects, extends, circular extends, TS18000, Circularity detected while resolving configuration, ExtendedSourceFiles, extendedConfigCache, OwnerCache.AddOwner, entry not found, panic

### 🕗 Version & Regression Information

- This is the behavior in every version I tried: `typescript@7.1.0-dev.20261014.1` (native binary `@typescript/typescript-linux-x64@7.1.0-dev.20261014.1`, built from `main` at 9eedcb0dd7257f8a1a175adbef28f3b6d3492d7a, which contains #64755) and a build of `main` at 257e87406c6a8be277cb8cd5ef8d7b458a650390, with the synchronous API (`typescript/sync`) on linux-x64, Node 26.12.0.
- It follows #64754. Since #64755, a project whose own `extends` chain is circular opens and reports TS18000, but a second project that extends into the same cycle now panics.

### ⏯ Playground Link

The API is not in the playground. The reproduction is one file.

### 💻 Code

`package.json`:

```json
{ "private": true, "type": "module", "dependencies": { "typescript": "7.1.0-dev.20261014.1" } }
```

`repro.mjs` writes `cycle/tsconfig.json`, which extends `cycle/a.json`, which extends `cycle/tsconfig.json` again, and `outer/tsconfig.json`, which extends `cycle/a.json`. It then opens one project after the other in one API session, in both orders:

```js
import { mkdirSync, rmSync, writeFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { join } from "node:path";
import { API } from "typescript/sync";

const dir = join(process.cwd(), "proj");
rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });
mkdirSync(join(dir, "cycle"), { recursive: true });
mkdirSync(join(dir, "outer"), { recursive: true });
writeFileSync(join(dir, "cycle", "tsconfig.json"), JSON.stringify({ extends: "./a.json" }));
writeFileSync(join(dir, "cycle", "a.json"), JSON.stringify({ extends: "./tsconfig.json" }));
writeFileSync(join(dir, "cycle", "main.ts"), "export const one = 1;\n");
writeFileSync(join(dir, "outer", "tsconfig.json"), JSON.stringify({ extends: "../cycle/a.json" }));
writeFileSync(join(dir, "outer", "main.ts"), "export const two = 2;\n");

for (const order of [["outer", "cycle"], ["cycle", "outer"]]) {
  const api = new API({ cwd: dir });
  try {
    api.createSnapshot({ openProjects: [join(dir, order[0], "tsconfig.json")] });
    const snapshot = api.createSnapshot({ openProjects: [join(dir, order[1], "tsconfig.json")] });
    const project = snapshot.getProjects().find((p) => p.configFileName.endsWith(`${order[1]}/tsconfig.json`));
    console.log(`${order.join(" then ")}:`, project.program.getConfigFileParsingDiagnostics().map((d) => `TS${d.code}: ${d.text}`));
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(`${order.join(" then ")}:`, String(error).split("\n")[0]);
  }
  api.close();
}
```

### 🙁 Actual behavior

Opening `outer` first works. Opening `cycle` first makes the second `createSnapshot` throw a Go panic, every time:

```text
$ node repro.mjs
outer then cycle: [ 'TS18000: Circularity detected while resolving configuration: {0}' ]
cycle then outer: Error: panic: OwnerCache.AddOwner: entry not found
$ cd proj && npx tsc -p outer; echo $?
error TS18000: Circularity detected while resolving configuration: {0}
2
```

The stack, trimmed to the relevant frames:

```text
github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/tsc/internal/project.(*OwnerCache[...]).AddOwner(0x377ff0dde2a0?, {0x377ff0dde2a0?, 0x2?}, 0x2?)
	github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/tsc/internal/project/ownercache.go:63 +0xe7
github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/tsc/internal/project.(*Snapshot).Clone(_, {_, _}, {{{0x0, 0x0, 0x0}, {0x0, 0x0, 0x0}, {0x0, ...}, ...}, ...}, ...)
	github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/tsc/internal/project/snapshot.go:717 +0x20a8
github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/tsc/internal/project.(*SnapshotHost).CloneSnapshot(0x0?, {0x1b41078?, 0x377ff0e94b00?}, 0x544780?, {{0x0, 0x0}, {0x0, 0x0}, {0x0}, {0x0}, ...}, ...)
	github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/tsc/internal/project/snapshothost.go:131 +0x27b
github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/tsc/internal/api.(*Session).handleCreateSnapshot(0x377ff0dc0000, {0x1b41078, 0x377ff0e94b00}, 0x377ff2543930)
	github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/tsc/internal/api/session.go:1440 +0x377
```

Opening both in one `createSnapshot({ openProjects: [cycle, outer] })` panics the same way in most runs (8 of 10 here), and occasionally (1 of 10) with `[outer, cycle]`, because the two projects load concurrently.

### 🙂 Expected behavior

Both projects open in either order, and each project's config file parsing diagnostics contain TS18000, as `tsc -p cycle` and `tsc -p outer` report.

### Additional information about the issue

**Impact:** a repository with a circular `extends` chain and another config that extends into it makes `createSnapshot` fail with a Go panic whenever the cyclic project was opened first, where `tsc -p` reports TS18000 for each project, so an analyzer that opens a repository's projects on the API fails on that repository depending on the order it opens them in.

[`Snapshot.Clone`](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/257e87406c6a8be277cb8cd5ef8d7b458a650390/tsc/internal/project/snapshot.go#L714-L720) calls `AddOwner` on the extended config cache for every path in each config's `ExtendedSourceFiles`, and [`AddOwner` panics](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/257e87406c6a8be277cb8cd5ef8d7b458a650390/tsc/internal/project/ownercache.go#L60-L63) when a path has no cache entry. Opening `cycle` parses `a.json` through the cache with `cycle/tsconfig.json` on the resolution stack. Inside it, `cycle/tsconfig.json` is already on the stack, so [`getExtendedConfig` parses it without the cache](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/257e87406c6a8be277cb8cd5ef8d7b458a650390/tsc/internal/tsoptions/tsconfigparsing.go#L910-L914), and the cached `a.json` entry lists `cycle/tsconfig.json` though nothing acquired an entry for it. Opening `outer` then finds `a.json` in the cache. The entry is [still valid](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/257e87406c6a8be277cb8cd5ef8d7b458a650390/tsc/internal/project/extendedconfigcache.go#L35-L52) because its hash covers only file contents, not the resolution stack it was parsed under, so `outer` inherits `cycle/tsconfig.json` in its `ExtendedSourceFiles`, and `Clone` panics on it. In the other order, `outer` parses `a.json` with only `outer/tsconfig.json` on the stack, so `cycle/tsconfig.json` goes through the cache and gets an entry.

The `{0}` left in the TS18000 text has a separate cause, as noted in #64754, and is not what this report is about.

I met this while building deadset-ts, the TypeScript analyzer of [deadset](https://github.com/cplieger/deadset), on the TypeScript 7 API. A pull request with the fix and a test follows.

