### 🔍 Search Terms

getAllDependencies, dependency graph, getResolvedModule, batched, 7.1 API, typescript/sync, typescript/async

### ✅ Viability Checklist

- [x] This wouldn't be a breaking change in existing TypeScript/JavaScript code
- [x] This wouldn't change the runtime behavior of existing JavaScript code
- [x] This could be implemented without emitting different JS based on the types of the expressions
- [x] This isn't a runtime feature (e.g. library functionality, non-ECMAScript syntax with JavaScript output, new syntax sugar for JS, etc.)
- [x] This isn't a request to add a new utility type: https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/wiki/No-New-Utility-Types
- [x] This feature would agree with the rest of our Design Goals: https://github.com/Microsoft/TypeScript/wiki/TypeScript-Design-Goals

### ⭐ Suggestion

In TypeScript ≤ 6, `BuilderProgram.getAllDependencies(sourceFile)` returned the transitive set of files a given source file depends on (module imports, `/// <reference path>`, type reference directives, and project-reference redirects).

With the 7.1 beta, builder/watch/solution-builder APIs are explicitly out of scope for stable (#64709), so there is no equivalent. The building blocks are mostly present (`program.getSourceFileNames()`, `program.getSourceFile()`, and the new `program.getResolvedModule()` / `getResolvedTypeReferenceDirective()` from #64247), but reconstructing the dependency graph requires **one IPC round-trip per import per file**, which gets expensive quickly on larger projects.

I'd like to request one (or both) of the following:

1. **A dependency-graph method on `Program`** — e.g. `program.getAllDependencies(sourceFile)` or `program.getDependencies(sourceFile)` returning the direct/transitive dependency file names, computed server-side.
2. **A batched resolution method** — e.g. `program.getResolvedModules(sourceFile)` returning all resolutions for a file in a single call, similar in spirit to the batched methods added in #63904. This would let consumers build the graph themselves with one call per file instead of one call per import.

Option 1 is the ergonomic fit for the old API; option 2 is a smaller primitive and can be used independently.


### 📃 Motivating Example

I use `getAllDependencies` in [StrykerJS](https://github.com/stryker-mutator/stryker-js) to identify inrelated files and use that to implement mutant grouping (a roughly 50% performance improvement over single mutant validation). Migrating to 7.1 currently means re-implementing the traversal client-side.

<details>
<summary>Current client-side reconstruction (7.1 beta) (using AI) </summary>

```ts
import { API } from "typescript/sync";

function getAllDependencies(program: Program, rootFileName: string) {
  const seen = new Set<string>();
  const queue = [rootFileName];

  while (queue.length) {
    const fileName = queue.pop()!;
    if (seen.has(fileName)) continue;
    seen.add(fileName);

    const sf = program.getSourceFile(fileName);
    if (!sf) continue;

    // One round-trip per import
    for (const imp of sf.imports) {
      const r = program.getResolvedModule(sf, imp.text /*, mode */);
      if (r?.resolvedModule) queue.push(r.resolvedModule.resolvedFileName);
    }

    // One round-trip per type reference directive
    for (const ref of sf.typeReferenceDirectives) {
      const r = program.getResolvedTypeReferenceDirective(sf, ref.fileName);
      if (r?.resolvedTypeReferenceDirective?.resolvedFileName) {
        queue.push(r.resolvedTypeReferenceDirective.resolvedFileName);
      }
    }

    for (const ref of sf.referencedFiles) {
      queue.push(resolve(dirname(fileName), ref.fileName));
    }
  }

  seen.delete(rootFileName);
  return [...seen];
}
```

</details>

Issues with this approach:

- Round-trip count scales with total import count, not file count.
- Project-reference output redirects (handled by the old builder method) have to be reimplemented manually.
- Resolution mode (`ESNext` vs `CommonJS`) must be derived client-side per import.



### 💻 Use Cases

1. **What do you want to use this for?**

Generate a list of unrelated files so we can validate multiple mutants in a single type-check run.

2. **What shortcomings exist with current approaches?**

Per-import IPC calls make the client-side traversal slow on large graphs, and project-reference redirects are easy to get subtly wrong.

3. **What workarounds are you using in the meantime?**

The reconstruction above.

